1260	`length` answers a text and a list, and it is the same function under each of the
  1261	three spellings: `length(xs)`, `text.length(xs)` and `list.length(xs)`. A record is
  1262	not a length — `length({a: 1})` is `RuntimeError: length requires a list or text`,
  1263	in all three spellings, and `tests/test_text.rb` pins the refusal as a caught
  1264	error. Both dotted spellings are module members, so both are written
  1265	above and in README.md — a name that answers without a document saying so is
  1266	surface nobody agreed to.
  1267	
  1268	`wrap` answers a list of lines no longer than the width. A word is moved to the
  1269	next line whole; only a word that is itself longer than the width is broken, into
  1270	width-sized pieces, so every line the function answers is within the width.
  1271	Existing newlines start a new line, CRLF is read as a line ending, and an empty
  1272	text wraps to an empty list. The width is a whole number of characters, at least
  1273	1; anything else is a catchable `RuntimeError` naming `wrap`.
  1274	
  1275	`diff` answers a **standard unified diff** between its two texts, with
  1276	`--- original` and `+++ changed` headers and three lines of context around each
  1277	change. Identical texts answer the empty text. A text whose last line has no
  1278	newline is written with the standard `\ No newline at end of file` marker, so
  1279	the output can be applied by any unified-diff reader rather than only by
  1280	Redblue.
  1281	
  1282	`dedent` removes the longest leading whitespace that every non-blank line
  1283	shares. A blank line does not vote, and a non-blank line with no leading
  1284	whitespace makes the shared prefix empty, which leaves every line exactly as it
  1285	was. Line endings are preserved, so CRLF input stays CRLF.
  1286	
  1287	`template` replaces `<<name>>` placeholders with fields of its record. The
  1288	delimiters are literal text rather than braces because `{…}` already means
  1289	interpolation inside a Redblue string. `<<<<` writes a literal `<<`, so a
  1290	template can contain its own opening delimiter; `>>` outside a placeholder needs
  1291	no escape. An unknown placeholder is a catchable `RuntimeError` that names the
  1292	missing field. Values are written with their usual text form, so a text is
  1293	inserted without quotes and a number as its number.
  1294	
  1295	`pad` right-pads a text to a width with a fill of one or more characters, and
  1296	`align` pads to a width on the named side: `"left"`, `"right"` or `"center"`.
  1297	Both count **characters**, not bytes, so padding `"é"` to 3 adds two characters
  1298	and padding `"日本"` to 5 adds three, regardless of their UTF-8 length. Text
  1299	already at or past the width is answered unchanged. An empty fill and an
  1300	unknown side are catchable errors naming the function.
  1301	
  1302	These six are spelled as module members only — `text.wrap`, `text.diff` and the rest — so a
  1303	bare `wrap(x, n)` remains an ordinary name a program may declare. The earlier text functions keep their flat spellings.
